men was originally created by Kelvin Rivera.

The project grew out of a series of personal AI systems developed by
Kelvin Rivera.

An early project, referred to retrospectively as Mach I, explored distributed
llama.cpp inference across the SER5 and PHENOM computers. It was abandoned
after real testing showed that PHENOM became a performance bottleneck rather
than an accelerator.

Kel AI, referred to retrospectively as Mach II, followed as a persistent
dual-mode personal AI system using Groq for online inference and a local
Qwen model for private/offline inference. Its UI, orchestration, retrieval,
privacy system, and surrounding architecture were built largely from scratch.
As a one-person project, its architecture and development commitment eventually
grew far beyond the original goal, and it too was dismantled.

Mach III was the architectural reset: a deliberately smaller local-first
personal AI assistant built around reuse of existing open-source components,
local WebLLM inference, persistent Markdown memory, web research, and a bounded
MCP tool interface.

men grew directly out of Mach III after a real MCP tool-result representation
failure demonstrated the need for a small deterministic evidence-normalization
layer between MCP tools and language models.

See PROVENANCE.md for the complete technical history, project lineage, and
extraction boundary.
